WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
- Today we're talking
about trusting your gut.

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.471
A lot of times your gut's
a pretty reliable source.

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:05.958
If I'm walking through the woods and I see

00:00:05.958 --> 00:00:07.920
a pack of wolves, my gut's gonna be like,

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.884
"Hey, run," and I would do that.

00:00:09.884 --> 00:00:12.250
But should you always stick
with your gut instinct?

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:13.213
- No!

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:14.818
- What?

00:00:14.818 --> 00:00:19.553
(funky music)

00:00:19.553 --> 00:00:21.364
To illustrate this point,
we're going to look at

00:00:21.364 --> 00:00:23.012
the Monty Hall Problem.

00:00:23.012 --> 00:00:23.907
Here's how it works.

00:00:23.907 --> 00:00:26.089
You're on a game show.
You've got three doors.

00:00:26.089 --> 00:00:27.830
Behind one, a great prize.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:30.212
Let's say it's a car,
like a really cool car.

00:00:30.212 --> 00:00:31.849
Behind the other two, sheep.

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:32.684
Oh what, you're gonna be one of

00:00:32.684 --> 00:00:33.568
those people who's all like,

00:00:33.568 --> 00:00:34.832
"Oh, a sheep's a good prize, anyway!"

00:00:34.832 --> 00:00:36.447
Well, fine, then it's a dead sheep.

00:00:36.447 --> 00:00:37.445
Oh God, that's upsetting.

00:00:37.445 --> 00:00:38.850
Fine, it's a live sheep. Undo!

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.183
So you've gotta pick one
out of the three doors.

00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:42.912
And we hit the streets
with a tiny game show to

00:00:42.912 --> 00:00:45.734
present the people with that exact choice.

00:00:45.734 --> 00:00:46.709
- Tiny Tiny Game Show!

00:00:46.709 --> 00:00:48.125
- Tiny Tiny Game Show.

00:00:48.125 --> 00:00:48.904
- It's so tiny.

00:00:48.904 --> 00:00:50.750
- Whoa!

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:55.686
- Behind one of these wonderful
doors is a brand new car!

00:00:55.686 --> 00:00:56.579
- Whoa!

00:00:56.579 --> 00:00:57.275
- Whoo!

00:00:57.275 --> 00:00:58.529
- What kind of car is it?

00:00:58.529 --> 00:01:00.316
- Well, you're gonna find out, aren't you?

00:01:00.316 --> 00:01:00.836
- Okay, okay.

00:01:00.836 --> 00:01:03.393
Behind two of the doors...

00:01:03.393 --> 00:01:04.080
is a sheep!

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.162
- What am I gonna do with a sheep?

00:01:05.162 --> 00:01:05.833
- I dunno.

00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:06.765
- I mean, in the right market,

00:01:06.765 --> 00:01:08.252
that could be worth
the same thing, though.

00:01:08.252 --> 00:01:09.525
- Yeah, well, you know.

00:01:09.525 --> 00:01:10.592
- You don't want a sheep.

00:01:10.592 --> 00:01:11.479
- You don't want a sheep!

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:12.963
- These sheep are sick.

00:01:12.963 --> 00:01:15.973
- So what I need you to
do is just pick a door.

00:01:15.973 --> 00:01:18.008
- I highly recommend door number one.

00:01:18.008 --> 00:01:20.224
Don't you dare!

00:01:20.224 --> 00:01:21.805
But also door number two's great.

00:01:21.805 --> 00:01:23.210
- I'm gonna go with door number one.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:24.046
- Number three.

00:01:24.046 --> 00:01:24.705
- Number one.

00:01:24.705 --> 00:01:25.810
- I think we're gonna go with number two.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:26.491
- Three.

00:01:26.491 --> 00:01:29.085
- You just gave us the "no,
don't pick three" look.

00:01:29.085 --> 00:01:30.385
- What are you talking about?

00:01:30.385 --> 00:01:31.105
- Three.

00:01:31.105 --> 00:01:31.643
- Three.

00:01:31.643 --> 00:01:32.163
- Three.

00:01:32.163 --> 00:01:33.345
- I'm gonna pick door number two.

00:01:33.345 --> 00:01:34.040
- Number three.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.529
- So let's look at the
odds of you winning.

00:01:35.529 --> 00:01:37.294
No matter which door you
choose from the start,

00:01:37.294 --> 00:01:38.843
you have a one in three chance of winning

00:01:38.843 --> 00:01:40.650
and a two in three chance of losing.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.891
Each door on its own offers
the same probability.

00:01:42.891 --> 00:01:44.646
So, you've picked your door
and crossed your fingers

00:01:44.646 --> 00:01:46.945
when suddenly, the game show host opens up

00:01:46.945 --> 00:01:48.524
one of the doors you haven't picked

00:01:48.524 --> 00:01:50.752
to reveal that one of
the sheep is behind it.

00:01:50.752 --> 00:01:52.830
And he gives you the option
to switch your choice.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.283
I'm gonna reveal to you...

00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:55.142
- Oh my gosh.

00:01:55.142 --> 00:01:56.384
- That behind door number three...

00:01:56.384 --> 00:01:57.127
- That's a sheep.

00:01:57.127 --> 00:01:58.457
- [Man] Sheep.
- [Host] ...is a sheep.

00:01:58.457 --> 00:01:59.131
- There's a dead sheep.

00:01:59.131 --> 00:01:59.897
- He fainted.

00:01:59.897 --> 00:02:00.582
- Oh!

00:02:00.582 --> 00:02:01.352
- That's what I'm talking about.

00:02:01.352 --> 00:02:05.115
- So knowing that, do you wanna
stick with door number three

00:02:05.115 --> 00:02:06.858
or switch to door number two?

00:02:06.858 --> 00:02:07.799
Should you switch?

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:10.978
Even if it feels like you're
betraying your gut instinct?

00:02:10.978 --> 00:02:12.523
Yeah! You should switch.

00:02:12.523 --> 00:02:13.359
Why?

00:02:13.359 --> 00:02:15.356
Well, what's important to
note is that if you combine

00:02:15.356 --> 00:02:17.503
the probability of two doors, you'd have

00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:18.864
a two in three chance of winning

00:02:18.864 --> 00:02:20.544
and a one in three chance of losing.

00:02:20.544 --> 00:02:22.321
So because the probabilities
have stayed the same,

00:02:22.321 --> 00:02:25.296
that door you picked initially
had a one in three chance

00:02:25.296 --> 00:02:27.443
of harboring a sweet new
ride, while the other two

00:02:27.443 --> 00:02:29.965
combined had a two in three chance.

00:02:29.965 --> 00:02:32.064
But now that one of
those two unpicked doors

00:02:32.064 --> 00:02:34.026
has been eliminated, that remaining door

00:02:34.026 --> 00:02:36.952
still has that same two in three chance.

00:02:36.952 --> 00:02:40.053
So by switching, you're
twice as likely to win.

00:02:40.053 --> 00:02:40.924
Does that make sense?

00:02:40.924 --> 00:02:43.304
Here, watch how better off
people fare when they switch.

00:02:43.304 --> 00:02:44.534
- I wanna pick number two.

00:02:44.534 --> 00:02:45.289
- You're switching to door number two?

00:02:45.289 --> 00:02:46.243
- I'm switching to door number two.

00:02:46.243 --> 00:02:47.309
- See what happens.

00:02:47.309 --> 00:02:48.830
You're gonna switch to two?

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.687
- You won the brand new car!

00:02:50.687 --> 00:02:51.884
- Whoa!

00:02:51.884 --> 00:02:52.319
- Whoa!

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:53.889
- It's a car!

00:02:53.889 --> 00:02:55.539
- Whoa!

00:02:55.539 --> 00:02:56.112
- Brand new!

00:02:56.112 --> 00:02:57.455
You won the brand new car!

00:02:57.455 --> 00:02:59.155
- A brand new car!

00:02:59.155 --> 00:03:00.118
- Is it the same sheep?

00:03:00.118 --> 00:03:01.086
- No, it's a different sheep.

00:03:01.086 --> 00:03:02.103
- [Host] And here's a supercut

00:03:02.103 --> 00:03:03.496
of all the losers who didn't switch.

00:03:03.496 --> 00:03:05.202
Let's delight in their misery.

00:03:05.202 --> 00:03:06.830
- I think I'm gonna stick to two.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:07.340
(sad music)

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:08.108
- You're gonna go with your gut?

00:03:08.108 --> 00:03:09.668
- You're sticking with door number three?

00:03:09.668 --> 00:03:10.526
(sheep bleat)

00:03:10.526 --> 00:03:11.346
- Aww, it's a sheep!

00:03:11.346 --> 00:03:12.089
- Aww, it's a sheep.

00:03:12.089 --> 00:03:13.110
- Son of a biscuit!

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.329
- Aww!

00:03:14.329 --> 00:03:15.062
- Oh no!

00:03:15.062 --> 00:03:17.083
- Aww, you got a sheep!

00:03:17.083 --> 00:03:18.478
(sheep bleat)

00:03:18.478 --> 00:03:20.545
- Aww!

00:03:20.545 --> 00:03:22.283
- You got a sheep!

00:03:22.283 --> 00:03:25.289
- When you first choose, it's
only one third of a chance.

00:03:25.289 --> 00:03:26.242
So you actually have a better chance

00:03:26.242 --> 00:03:29.110
when you select a new door.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:30.269
- See, that lady gets it.

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:31.628
Now if it still doesn't make sense to you,

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:33.021
let's blow the whole thing up and say

00:03:33.021 --> 00:03:34.949
that you were presented with 50 doors.

00:03:34.949 --> 00:03:35.888
You pick one.

00:03:35.888 --> 00:03:37.986
The host then opens 48 doors and

00:03:37.986 --> 00:03:39.546
gives you the chance to switch.

00:03:39.546 --> 00:03:42.010
Are you gonna stick with that
one random door you picked

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.991
or switch to the one door
that the host didn't open?

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:46.830
Makes a little more sense? Kinda, yeah?

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.348
So, the next time you find yourself

00:03:48.348 --> 00:03:51.731
in one of those classic three-door
scenarios, breathe easy.

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:52.727
You've got this.

00:03:52.727 --> 00:03:54.608
And that's some cool science for ya.

00:03:54.608 --> 00:03:56.046
- It's cool. It's math, but I think--

00:03:56.046 --> 00:03:57.540
- Ah, you're right. Well...

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:58.560
- It's fine.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.853
- It's still a subject in school.

00:03:59.853 --> 00:04:02.649
(both make random noises)

